/-
# I clicked Save again and got two drafts

My form said "Receipt received" after the second click. That looked fine:
I'd clicked Save once, got a network error, and tried again. Then I checked
the server. It had saved "Release checklist" both times. On a fresh run I
got the same two messages, but this time the second click really was necessary.

Run `haze submit_receipt.hz` locally, or edit the blocks on this page.
To repeat the browser experiment, run `python submit_receipt.py after` and
open the address it prints. Click Save twice. Restart with `before` to drop
the first write, or with `after --keyed` to recognize the retry.
-/

/-
## What the browser saw

I made a [form with a Python server](../Conformance/submit_receipt.py)
so I could disconnect it at a line of my choosing. There's a list of
drafts in memory. On the first POST, the server can hang up just before
it adds the draft to the list, or just after. Neither setting sends an
HTTP response. In both runs `fetch` throws and the page shows
`No receipt (TypeError)`. The next POST goes through normally.

| Where I closed the connection | Drafts after one click | Drafts after two |
|---|---:|---:|
| Before saving | 0 | 1 |
| After saving | 1 | 2 |

I got those counts from `/audit` in another tab. The error handler only
has the exception. That's also what a [browser agent](https://browyhq.github.io/blog/how-i-built-browy/)
has to work with on a site that doesn't offer a way to look up this save.
From there, retrying makes sense if the list is empty. If the draft is
already there, the sensible thing is to leave it alone.

I wanted to know what the handler could promise without that lookup.
It gets one more POST if it wants one, and that POST is certain to arrive.
For a starting point I had it toss a fair coin: retry on heads, stop on
tails. In the code, `cut` is a server setting I chose before the click.
The two `1`s mean that Haze must allow either setting. The only odds I
gave it were the odds of retrying.

<!-- run -->

With the connection cut before the write, heads leaves one draft. With it
cut after the write, tails leaves one draft. That's why Haze can print a
single `0.5` without knowing which cut is more common.
-/

enum Cut = BeforeWrite | AfterWrite
param cut : Cut ~= {BeforeWrite: 1, AfterWrite: 1}

def copies(retry : Bool) : Num :=
  (if cut == AfterWrite then 1 else 0) + (if retry then 1 else 0)

def exactly_one(p : Num) : Bool := copies(flip(p)) == 1
#prob exactly_one(0.5)

/-
## Retrying nine times out of ten

I changed the retry rate to 90%. If the first write never happened, the
handler now saves the draft nine times out of ten. But consider the other
run. The first click *did* save the draft, and now the handler duplicates
it nine times out of ten.

You can change `0.9` to `0.8` below. The chance of a single draft goes up
in one run by exactly as much as it goes down in the other.

<!-- run -->

Call the chance of retrying `p`. If the first POST didn't save the draft,
only a retry can leave one copy, so the chance is `p`. If the first POST
did save it, only stopping can leave one copy, so the chance is `1 - p`.
Those numbers add to one. Any policy that beats the fair coin in one run
has to do worse in the other.

![For retry chance p, success rises from zero to one if the first write was lost and falls from one to zero if it was saved. The two lines meet at one half.](https://haze-lang.pages.dev/blog/images/submit-retry.svg)
At the crossing, a fair coin gives one copy half the time on either server setting.

On a real service I'd want to know which failure usually happens. Say the
logs show twenty missing replies. Nineteen of those drafts were already
saved. The fair coin still gives a 50% chance of one copy in each case,
but now there's a reason to put the coin away. If the handler had stopped
on all twenty errors, it would have been right 95% of the time. The one
draft that hadn't been saved would still be missing. I can't estimate
how often that happens from my experiment. I ran each setting once on purpose.
-/

#prob exactly_one(0.9)

/-
## The key was there on the first click

The two POSTs that made the duplicate both carried `Idempotency-Key: draft-1`.
By default my server appends the draft even if it has seen that key before.
I restarted it with `after --keyed` and tried the same two clicks. This time it
found `draft-1` on the second POST and sent a receipt without adding another
row. I hadn't taught the browser to tell the runs apart. I'd made a second
click safe in either run.

A new draft needs a new key. In the fixture I keep the list and the keys
in memory under one lock. If I moved the drafts to a database but left
the keys in Python, a restart between clicks would preserve the first
draft and forget its key. I'd want to commit the two together and keep
the key for as long as the client can retry.

Of course, I could also promise never to save two copies by never saving
anything. So alongside `always rows <= 1` I checked whether the server
had *at least one* draft after two retries. This time the client sends
both. Each has an independent 90% chance of arriving. Change
`retry_delivery` to `0.5` to check a worse connection.

<!-- run -->

If the first POST didn't write the draft, both retries have to miss for
the list to stay empty. At 90% delivery the chance of those two misses
is 1%; at 50% it's 25%. If the first POST did write the draft,
it's still there even when neither retry gets through. In neither case
can a retry add a second copy.

The other way out is to find out what happened to that particular save.
A receipt for `draft-1` would settle it. An empty list wouldn't, if the
first POST might still be running. That's why [HTTP's retry guidance](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-9.2.2)
advises against automatically retrying a non-idempotent request unless
the client knows it wasn't applied or knows the effect won't be repeated.

On the original form I'd put "Couldn't confirm the save" next to the
button. That's what the browser knows. I'd only add "Try again" once I
knew what the server would do with the second click.
-/

system Save(dedup : Bool, delivery : Num) {
  var seen_key : Bool := cut == AfterWrite
  var rows : Num := if cut == AfterWrite then 1 else 0
  step {
    if flip(delivery) {
      if not (dedup and seen_key) { rows := rows + 1 }
      seen_key := true
    }
  }
}

def retry_delivery : Num := 0.9
theorem no_duplicate : Save(true, retry_delivery) |= always rows <= 1
#prob Save(true, retry_delivery) |= after[2] rows >= 1
